<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernardo O'Higgins</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernardo O'Higgins was a leader of Chilean independence and the son of an Irish-born viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> he defeated the royalists at Chacabuco in 1817 and Maipú in 1818. As Supreme Director he signed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>'s declaration of independence in 1818, but his authoritarian rule led to his resignation in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins.jpg" alt="Bernardo O'Higgins" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Gil de Castro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohiggins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernardo O'Higgins was born on 20 August 1778 in Chillán, the illegitimate son of Ambrosio O'Higgins, a Spanish officer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-sligo">County Sligo</a> who became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and later viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and of Isabel Riquelme. His father never acknowledged him, and the two never met in person, though Ambrosio supported him financially. At seventeen he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he met the Venezuelan Francisco de Miranda and joined the Lautaro Lodge, a society dedicated to the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. After his father's death in 1801 he inherited a large estate and returned to Chile in 1802 as a gentleman farmer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 18 September 1810 O'Higgins joined the revolt that created the Government Junta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, a date now Chile's Independence Day. The patriots were split between the Carrera family and the circle of O'Higgins in Concepción. In October 1814 O'Higgins garrisoned the main square of Rancagua rather than the gorge he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a> had agreed on; Carrera did not arrive with reinforcements, and the royalists won. O'Higgins broke out with a few men and spent three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, assembled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> and returned to Chile in 1817 to win at Chacabuco.</p><p>He was granted dictatorial powers as Supreme Director on 16 February 1817 after San Martín declined the post, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> proclaimed itself independent on 12 February 1818. He founded the Chilean Military Academy and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a>, the modern Chilean Navy, and sent an expedition to liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. His proposals to establish democracy and abolish titles of nobility met resistance from landowners, and he offended the Church and then the business community. The government went bankrupt, and Carrera's execution in 1821 damaged his name. A conservative coup deposed him on 28 January 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>O'Higgins left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> in July 1823 and never saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s government granted him two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> but gave him no command. In 1842 the National Congress of Chile voted to allow his return, but he fell ill with heart trouble at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 24 October 1842. His remains were brought to Chile in 1869 and now lie in the Crypt of the Liberator. A region of Chile, a national park and the main avenue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernardo O'Higgins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a governing junta formed on 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as the national holiday, opening the period known as the Patria Vieja, in which creole leaders such as José Miguel Carrera and <b>Bernardo O'Higgins</b> quarrelled while Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal sent royalist forces from Peru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bernardo O'Higgins</b> formally proclaimed Chile's independence on the first anniversary of the victory at Chacabuco.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-12">12 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Royalist forces regained control in 1814, but José de San Martín and <b>Bernardo O'Higgins</b> won at Chacabuco in 1817 and Maipú in 1818, when Chile declared independence as the Republic of Chile.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-chile">Captaincy General of Chile</a></li><li><blockquote>Divisions between the Carreras and <b>Bernardo O'Higgins</b> weakened the patriots as the viceroy of Peru, José Fernando de Abascal, sent troops in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/state/patria-vieja">Patria Vieja</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chillán, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-36.61,-72.1&amp;year=1819" aria-label="Chillán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2739 3483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bernardo O'Higgins"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3189 3753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3189 3753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-36.61,-72.1&amp;year=1819">What was here in 1819? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Although we are in a minority we shall know how to supply that defect by our energy and our courage; we are sufficient to oppose effectually the delivery of this money, of which our country threatened with invasion has need.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the Chilean Congress, 1811 &#183; tr. William Pilling, 1893 &#183; as quoted in Bartolomé Mitre's history, The Emancipation of South America &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48856/pg48856-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The cause which Chile defends is the same in which Buenos Ayres, New Granada, Mexico, and Venezuela are engaged; it is that of the whole Continent of America.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Simón Bolívar, 1818 &#183; tr. William Pilling, 1893 &#183; as quoted in Bartolomé Mitre's history, The Emancipation of South America &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48856/pg48856-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
